function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:`${e.marker}) ${r}`}})}var t={id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-1`,text:`Super realistic masks are made from flexible materials such as silicone and are designed to imitate real human faces — down to every last detail. In a study by the Universities of York and Kyoto, researchers asked participants to look at pairs of photographs and decide which showed a`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-2`,text:`__26__ face and which showed a person wearing a mask. Surprisingly, participants made the wrong call in one-in-five cases. The 20% error rate`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-3`,text:`__27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-4`,text:`__28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab. The researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-5`,text:`__29__ any differences according to race. When trial participants were asked to choose between photographs of faces of a different race from theirs, response times were slower and selections were 5% less`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-6`,text:`__30__ . There are now dozens of criminal cases in which offenders have`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-7`,text:`__31__ themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender, sending police investigations down the wrong path. In one recent case, an international gang used an`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-8`,text:`__32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister,`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-9`,text:`__33__ business executives out of millions of pounds. Dr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: “Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-10`,text:`__34__ the key characteristics of a person’s appearance to be`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-11`,text:`__35__ identified.”`}]},n={id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp`,title:`Learning vs. Practicing: Why Knowledge Alone Is Not Enough`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Most people have a dream in life, a vision of who or what they’d like to be in the future. At a minimum, everyone has interests and values that determine what they want out of their lives. Even so, trying to set achievable goals that you’ll work for over the course of many years can be extremely challenging. It can be hard to know where to even begin, and the things you hope to achieve may seem impossible. But, if you’re well prepared you may be able to set goals for your life that are just as fulfilling to work toward as to achieve.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`We all have goals that we want to achieve in our lives. These goals may include learning a new language, eating healthier and losing weight, becoming a better parent, saving more money, and so on. It can be easy to assume that the gap between where you are now and where you want to be in the future is caused by a lack of knowledge. This is why we buy courses on how to start a business or how to lose weight fast or how to learn a new language in three months. We assume that if we knew about a better strategy, then we would get better results. We believe that a new result requires new knowledge.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`What I’m starting to realize, however, is that new knowledge does not necessarily drive new results. In fact, learning something new can actually be a waste of time if your goal is to make progress and not simply gain additional knowledge.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`It all comes down to the difference between learning and practicing. Thomas Sterner, in his book The Practicing Mind, explains the key difference between practicing and learning. He says: “When we practice something, we are involved in the deliberate repetition of a process with the intention of reaching a specific goal.” Here the words “deliberate” and “intention” are key, because they define the difference between actively practicing something and passively learning it.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Learning something new and practicing something new may seem very similar, but these two methods can have profoundly different results. Here are some additional ways to think about the difference. Let’s say your goal is to get stronger and more fit. You can research the best instructions on weight lifting technique, but the only way to build strength is to practice lifting weights. Let’s say your goal is to grow your startup. You can learn about the best way to make a sales pitch, but the only way to actually land customers is to practice making sales calls. Let’s say your goal is to write a book. You can talk to a best-selling author about writing, but the only way to become a better writer is to practice publishing consistently.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Passive learning creates knowledge. Active practice creates skill. Let’s consider a couple more reasons to prioritize active practice over passive learning. First, passive learning can be a stick that supports inaction. In many cases, learning is actually a way to avoid taking action on the goals and interests that we say are important to us. For example, let’s say you want to learn a foreign language. Reading a book on how to learn a foreign language quickly allows you to feel like you are making progress (“Hey, I’m figuring out the best way to do this!”). Of course, you’re not actually practicing the action that would deliver your desired outcome (speaking the foreign language).`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`In situations like this one, we often claim that we are preparing or researching the best method, but these rationalizations allow us to feel like we are moving forward when we are merely spinning our wheels. We make the mistake of being in motion rather than taking action. Learning is valuable until it becomes a form of procrastination (拖延) .`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Another point to consider is that practice is learning, but learning is not practice. Passive learning is not a form of practice because although you gain new knowledge, you are not discovering how to apply that knowledge. Active practice, meanwhile, is one of the greatest forms of learning because the mistakes you make while practicing reveal important insights.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Even more importantly, practice is the only way to make a meaningful contribution with your knowledge. You can watch an online course about how to build a business or read an article about a terrible disaster in a developing nation, but that knowledge is unproductive unless you actually launch your business or donate to those in need. Learning by itself can be valuable for you, but if you want to be valuable to others, then you have to express your knowledge in some way.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The last important point to consider is that practice focuses your energy on the process. Thomas Sterner once said, “Progress is a natural result of staying focused on the process of doing anything.” The state of your life right now is a result of the habits and beliefs that you have been practicing each day. When you realize this and begin to direct your focus toward practicing better habits day-in and day-out, continual progress will be the logical outcome. It is not the things we learn nor the dreams we visualize that determine our results, but rather the habits that we practice each day. Fall in love with boredom and focus your energy on the process, not the product.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`What does this all mean? Is passive learning useless? Of course not. In many cases, learning for the sake of learning can be a beautiful thing. Not to mention that soaking up new information can help you make more informed decisions when you do decide to take action. That said, the main point of this article is that learning by itself does not lead to progress. We often hide behind information and use learning as an excuse to delay the more difficult and more important choice of actually doing something. Spend less time passively learning and more time actively practicing. Stop thinking and start doing.`}]},r={"cet4-2024-12-s3":{subtitle:`2024 年 12 月 · 第 3 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2024年12月英语四级第3套真题，写作考如何为绿色校园做贡献，翻译考中国新能源汽车产业发展，选词填空考超仿真面具与人脸识别，长篇阅读考学习与练习的差别，仔细阅读为自动驾驶法律责任与蛋白质摄入迷思；听力给出 8 段原文并由站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on how the students can contribute to a green campus. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`A green campus depends on the daily choices of everyone who studies and works there. As students, we can make a real difference by turning environmental awareness into practical action.

First, we should save resources in our everyday lives. Switching off lights when leaving a classroom, taking shorter showers and printing on both sides of paper are simple habits that reduce waste. Second, we can choose a greener way of living. Walking or cycling around campus and carrying reusable cups and bags can cut pollution and plastic waste. We should also sort rubbish carefully and avoid ordering more food than we can eat.

Finally, students can work together through environmental clubs and campus activities. By sharing useful ideas and encouraging our friends to participate, we can make sustainable habits more common. Small actions, when continued by many people, will help create a cleaner and more pleasant campus.`,guidance:[`开篇点题：首句 "A green campus depends on the daily choices of everyone who studies and works there" 直接回应"学生如何为绿色校园做贡献"，用 depends on the daily choices 把"绿色校园"落到日常选择上；次句 "As students, we can make a real difference by turning environmental awareness into practical action" 亮明主旨：把环保意识转化为实际行动。`,`主体第一层（节约资源）："First, we should save resources in our everyday lives" 后接 Switching off lights when leaving a classroom、taking shorter showers、printing on both sides of paper 三个具体习惯，并用 "simple habits that reduce waste" 点明效果——这是"随手可做"的节能行动。`,`主体第二层（绿色生活）："Second, we can choose a greener way of living" 提出 Walking or cycling around campus、carrying reusable cups and bags 可 cut pollution and plastic waste，并补充分类垃圾与按需点餐，用 "Second" 与 First 构成递进的并列结构。`,`结尾升华（协作与总结）："Finally, students can work together through environmental clubs and campus activities"，借 "Small actions, when continued by many people, will help create a cleaner and more pleasant campus" 以"小行动、多坚持"收束全文。全文约 165 词，符合 120–180 词要求。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on how the students can contribute to a green campus. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`命题作文：就学生如何为绿色校园做贡献写一篇 120–180 词短文。参考范文开篇用 depends on the daily choices 点题；主体以 First（关灯、缩短淋浴、双面打印等节约资源）、Second（步行或骑行、自带杯袋、垃圾分类）两层并列展开；结尾用 Finally 引出环保社团协作，并以 Small actions, when continued by many people 收束。结构为"点题—双层主体论据—协作升华"，词数约 165。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear three news reports. At the end of each news report, you will hear two or three questions. Both the news reports and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A, B, C and D, then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`News Report 1 Across the US, families are looking to the winter with fear as energy costs soar. A number of factors are creating a bad situation. Global energy consumption is up, but supplies have been reduced. The Energy Department projects heating bills will jump 28% this winter, for those who rely on natural gas. Natural gas is used by nearly half of US households for heat. Heating oil is projected to be 27% higher and electricity 10% higher. That comes against inflation rates that accelerated last month, with consumer prices growing 6.6%. That's the fastest pace in four decades. The pain will be especially acute in parts of the country that heavily rely on heating oil to keep homes warm. It's projected to cost more than $2,300 to heat a typical home with heating oil this winter. Last month, the federal government added $1 billion in funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, bringing the total to $4.8 billion. But that represents a reduction from last year's energy assistance package of over $8 billion.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`News Report 2 Many Americans think they make healthy food choices, but they may be viewing their diet too optimistically. That's the main finding of a new study by researchers at the US Department of Agriculture in Stoneville, Mississippi. The study aimed to identify disconnects between how healthily Americans think they eat and how they actually do. For the study, the researchers asked participants to rate their diet as excellent, very good, good, fair, or poor. Folks also completed 24-hour food questionnaires. Then, the researchers compared answers to see how well responses to the two exercises matched up. Of more than 9,700 people, roughly 85 % were off-base when asked to rate the quality of their diet, and almost all overestimated how healthy it was. Those who rated their diet as poor tended to be far more accurate. Their rating matched that of the researchers more than 9 times out of 10.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`News Report 3 It was a heartwarming moment that dozens of Indian rescuers rallied together to use a crane in order to save an elephant trapped at the bottom of a 20 -foot well. The massive rescue effort took place last week close to an army camp. The elephant wandered out of the jungle and fell into the well. The army officers heard a noise coming from the well and realized an elephant was trapped deep inside. The officers then threw in some food for the creature and called the local forest department for help. Video of the rescue operation shows how the team used a crane to lift up the huge animal out of the well and onto the back of a truck. It was then taken to an examining room for evaluation of any injuries. The elephant was kept under close supervision for a few days before it was successfully released back into the wild.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`Conversation 1 I need to stop at the bank to withdraw some cash. Why not just pay with your phone? Well, actually, I've stopped using online payments for the past three months now. Stopped paying online? Oh, you must be worried that online payments aren't secure. No, they are safe. But paying online is too convenient and I spent too much money on things I didn't need. Oh, impulse purchases can be an issue for me too. I've definitely bought clothes and shoes I didn't need because they were on sale and paying with my phone was so easy. But I've found that if I review my spending every night, that keeps me from spending too much. I tried that strategy, but it just didn't work because I'd be busy studying all day. Then when I was tired and hungry, I'd use my phone to order food like burgers and other junk instead of going to the canteen where I could get a healthier meal for half the price. Well, what do you do now then? It seems like you'd have the same problems with cash. I go to the bank once a week and the rest of the time I keep my bank card at home so I can't get more cash out. And I also have a notebook where I record my expenses. But what prevents you from just going to the bank and getting more money if you want to buy something? The first month I did withdraw extra cash twice, but I felt guilty about it and after that I refused to spend more than what I had budgeted. By now, it's just a habit.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`Conversation 2. Please hear me out. I don't want our son to spend his afternoons doing something he hates, even if it might prepare him for the future. What about getting him to join a sport like soccer? From all the running and training he could become quite athletic, not to mention the great physical benefits from exercising outside every day. I don't think he should be doing something so challenging with all his breathing issues. What about playing an indoor sport like badminton? The environment is easier to control and he can still get a good workout, in addition to improving his hand-eye coordination. Besides, I could teach him some of the basics from my time as a badminton player. I'm thinking team sports should be the way to go. There's nothing like playing as part of a unit to accomplish a goal like winning a game or even a championship. He could try basketball since many of the courts are indoors now and it's a sport that could help him understand team spirit while cooperating with others. Maybe he could even join the school basketball team when he's ready. Oh, oh, what about volleyball? It's a team sport and they often have mixed-gender teams so he could socialize with both girls and boys. It's not as popular as some sports, which means he has better odds of possibly getting an athletic scholarship in the future. I also believe you were a beach volleyball athlete yourself not so long ago. As I've said many times before, beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are completely different.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Passage 1. Liftoff is usually the most environmentally harmful stage of any space mission, with vast quantities of fuel burnt up in a matter of minutes. For instance, SpaceX's Falcon 9 gets through 112 tons of refined fuel, emitting about 336 tons of CO2. This is the equivalent produced by your average car driving almost 70 times around the world. As well as greenhouse gases, rocket engines emit particles that destroy ozone. These issues are growing more pressing with the emergence of commercial spaceflight. There were 114 space launches in 2020, but there may be up to 1,000 per year in future. Sustainable fuels are the top priority to enable greener space travel. Current spacecraft use a variety of fuels, but most are based on fossil fuels. One potentially greener option is liquid hydrogen and oxygen. Hydrogen can be obtained sustainably by using solar power to break water down into oxygen and hydrogen. Reusable rockets can cut down on some of the waste associated with spaceflight. Traditionally, boosters, fuel tanks and other components are treated as expendable, but guiding them back to Earth in a controlled manner opens new possibilities. Most components from the Falcon 9 can be reused up to 100 times. Truly environmentally friendly space travel is still some way off, but we already have many of the technologies needed to start limiting its impact on our planet.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`Passage 2. Helping to look after a pet can facilitate numerous aspects of a child's emotional development. Research shows that reading to a loyal and non-judgmental companion, such as a dog or cat, can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud, which will in turn boost their self-confidence. Having a companion to talk to about their feelings can also help children feel secure and develop communication skills. A household pet can also cultivate a child's sense of responsibility as their parent explains the necessary processes of keeping their pet happy and healthy. As a child matures, they can then take on more of the daily responsibilities, boosting their sense of independence. Pets also provide a fantastic subject for research and learning. Parents can encourage their child to research their favorite pets at the library or during a supervised internet search. Taking children along to the pet shop to buy food and supplies will support their cognitive development as their passion for learning flourishes. A child's physical development can benefit greatly from taking pets for walks in the fresh air. Even their fine motor skills can be utilized in taking care of a beloved animal. When responsibilities are well paced and communicated, a child can benefit greatly from involvement in bringing up a happy and healthy pet. And of course, if a child doesn't have a pet in their own household, they can still benefit from all of the above when visiting a friend who does.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`Passage 3. A new bill in the Ohio legislature would require state public colleges and universities to send prospective students a packet of information. This would include costs of college, grants, and scholarships. It would also include expected monthly loan payments and projected salary after college. State Representative Adam Matthews is one of the two legislators who proposed the bill. He said it would give prospective students more information about what to expect once they graduate. Putting that information in front of them will give them the best opportunity to match their skills and their dreams to what our schools can provide, said Matthews. He notes that the state can only regulate public institutions in this way, but he hopes private institutions in Ohio follow the lead of the state and post similar information. He believes giving out this information will help Ohio's colleges and universities to be more competitive. The information will highlight what a great value they have for their very reasonable cost. Ohio's schools are already competitive. The state attracts more undergraduate students than it sends to colleges out of state. Many colleges and universities currently provide similar information on their websites. This includes tuition, fees, graduation rates, and more. But students need to search for the information. This bill would require the universities and colleges to send a packet directly to students.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-1`,stem:`Why are families across the US looking to this winter with fear?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They will see a 27% higher price to buy natural gas.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They will have insufficient heating oil to keep warm.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They will have a costly bill to pay to heat their homes.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They will experience the coldest winter in four decades.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "The Energy Department projects heating bills will jump 28% this winter, for those who rely on natural gas. Natural gas is used by nearly half of US households for heat"——能源部预测依赖天然气供暖的家庭取暖账单将上涨 28%，且近一半美国家庭用天然气取暖，故选 C（取暖费用高昂）。A 项 27% 是取暖油（heating oil）的涨幅，B 项与"取暖油不够"无关，D 项"四十年最冷"原文只说通胀增速创四十年最快，并非寒冬。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-1`,stem:`What did the federal government do last month?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Provided additional funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Assured U.S. citizens of an increase in energy supplies by the end of this year.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Predicted a 10% jump in heating bills for most U.S. households this winter.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Decided on this year's energy assistance package of $8 billion in total.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Last month, the federal government added $1 billion in funding to Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, bringing the total to $4.8 billion"——联邦政府上月向低收入家庭能源援助计划追加了 10 亿美元拨款，选 A；D 项 80 亿美元是"去年"的援助总额（a reduction from last year's energy assistance package of over $8 billion），不是今年的决定。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-2`,stem:`What is the main finding of a new study about many Americans?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They care less about their diet now than before.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They may lack adequate knowledge of healthy food.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They may hold too optimistic a view about their diet.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They pay insufficient attention to their choice of food.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文首句 "Many Americans think they make healthy food choices, but they may be viewing their diet too optimistically. That's the main finding of a new study"——新研究的主要发现是美国人可能"对自己的饮食过于乐观"，选 C；A 项与原文"总体高估健康程度"相反，B、D 项并非研究发现。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-2`,stem:`Who tended to rate their diet more accurately in the survey?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Those who finished 24-hour food questionnaires.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Those who fully understood the rating scale.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Those who cared about food quality.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Those who rated their diet as poor.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "Those who rated their diet as poor tended to be far more accurate. Their rating matched that of the researchers more than 9 times out of 10"——自评饮食"较差"的人评估准确度更高，选 D；A、B 项只是调查中使用的两种记录方式，并非准确度更高的群体。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What does the report say dozens of Indians did last week?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They experienced a heartbreaking moment.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They gathered together to rescue an elephant.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They heard a noise from the bottom of a well.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They found an elephant wandering around a well.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "It was a heartwarming moment that dozens of Indian rescuers rallied together to use a crane in order to save an elephant trapped at the bottom of a 20-foot well"——数十名印度人聚集用吊车营救困在井底的大象，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`Why did the army officers call the local forest department?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To borrow their crane.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To ask them for help.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To report the strange noise.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To get food for the elephant.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "The officers then threw in some food for the creature and called the local forest department for help"——军官们向井里投食后打电话给当地森林部门求助，选 B（请求协助）；C 项"报告异常声响"是此前听到声音，不是打电话的原因。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What does the news report say happened finally?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The elephant was able to return to the jungle.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The army officers were examined for injuries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The army officers were rewarded for their rescue efforts.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The elephant was given medical treatment for many days.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "The elephant was kept under close supervision for a few days before it was successfully released back into the wild"——大象最终被成功放归野外，选 A；D 项"接受多日治疗"只对应"带到检查室评估伤情并密切看护几天"，并非最终结局。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`Why has the man stopped paying online for the last three months?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He found it had caused him much inconvenience.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It led him to spend more money than necessary.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was much less secure than paying with cash.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He wanted to be less dependent on his phone.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "But paying online is too convenient and I spent too much money on things I didn't need"——男士停用线上支付是因为太方便、导致他花掉更多钱买不需要的东西，选 B；C 项被明确否定（"No, they are safe"）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What problem does the woman say she may also have?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Living beyond her means.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Lack of budgeting strategies.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Fashion addiction.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Impulse purchasing.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "Oh, impulse purchases can be an issue for me too. I've definitely bought clothes and shoes I didn't need because they were on sale and paying with my phone was so easy"——女士说自己也有"冲动消费"问题，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What does the man say he could do at the canteen?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Eat healthier food with less money.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Order food like burgers and chips.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Avoid getting addicted to junk food.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay either in cash or with his phone.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "I'd use my phone to order food like burgers and other junk instead of going to the canteen where I could get a healthier meal for half the price"——男士说去食堂能花一半价钱吃到更健康的餐食，选 A；B 项恰是他想避免的做法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What is now just a habit for the man?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Making a shopping list.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sticking to his budget.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Withdrawing cash only once a month.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Thinking twice before buying something.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "I felt guilty about it and after that I refused to spend more than what I had budgeted. By now, it's just a habit"——现在"严守预算"对他已成习惯，选 B；D 项"三思后再买"是女士的做法（review my spending every night），非男士。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`Why is the woman opposed to their son joining a sport like soccer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He does not enjoy any ball games.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He has to prepare for the future.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He has breathing problems.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He is not up to anything challenging.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "I don't think he should be doing something so challenging with all his breathing issues"——女士反对是因为儿子有呼吸问题，选 C；A 项与"team sports should be the way to go"的男士主张相反。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What does the man suggest their son try in order to understand team spirit?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Soccer.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Badminton.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Volleyball.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Basketball.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "He could try basketball since many of the courts are indoors now and it's a sport that could help him understand team spirit while cooperating with others"——男士建议篮球以体会团队精神，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What is one of the woman's considerations in choosing a sport for her son?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The possibilities of joining the school sports team.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The opportunities of winning some championships.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The chances of getting an athletic scholarship later on.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The odds of becoming a professional sportsman someday.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "It's not as popular as some sports, which means he has better odds of possibly getting an athletic scholarship in the future"——女士考虑的是日后获得体育奖学金的机会，选 C；D 项"成为职业运动员"原文未提。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What does the man say about beach volleyball?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It differs entirely from indoor volleyball.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It appeals to both boys and girls.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is less popular than indoor volleyball.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is completely different from other outdoor games.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "As I've said many times before, beach volleyball and indoor volleyball are completely different"——男士说沙滩排球与室内排球"完全不同"，选 A；D 项"与其他户外运动完全不同"是对原文的过度引申，原文只与室内排球对比。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about the liftoff of spacecraft?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It usually requires years of preparations.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It does a lot of harm to the environment.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It often burns over three hundred tonnes of refined fuel.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It proves to be the most complicated stage of space missions.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Liftoff is usually the most environmentally harmful stage of any space mission, with vast quantities of fuel burnt up in a matter of minutes"——发射阶段对环境危害最大，选 B；C 项 300 吨是 CO₂ 排放量而非燃烧的精炼燃料量。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What happens traditionally to boosters, fuel tanks and other components in spaceflight?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are burnt up in outer space.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are guided back to Earth.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are reused up to 100 times.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are treated as expendable.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "Traditionally, boosters, fuel tanks and other components are treated as expendable"——助推器与燃料舱等传统上被当作"一次性用品"，选 D；A、B 两项是"受控带回地面"这一新设想的效果，不是传统做法。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What does the passage say we already have regarding spaceflight?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) New technologies for bringing back space vehicles for recycling.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The technology for meeting the needs of commercial space travel.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Many of the technologies to limit the impact of space travel on Earth.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The technology for effectively protecting zones from being destroyed.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "we already have many of the technologies needed to start limiting its impact on our planet"——我们已拥有许多可以限制航天活动对地球影响的技术，选 C；A、D 项原文均未涉及（回收航天器、保护区域免遭破坏）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`What does research show about reading to a loyal and non-judgmental companion?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It can help unskilled readers feel more secure.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It can strengthen children's confidence in others.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It can boost children's interest in communication.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "reading to a loyal and non-judgmental companion, such as a dog or cat, can encourage reluctant readers to read aloud, which will in turn boost their self-confidence"——朗读能鼓励不爱阅读的孩子大声读出来，选 B；A 项"让不熟练的读者更安心"对应的是"有对象倾诉情绪则更有安全感"，并非朗读的效果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`How can parents make pets a fantastic subject for their children's research and learning?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By motivating children to find out about their favourite pets.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By taking children to the library to borrow books on animals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By allowing children to surf the internet about pets by themselves.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By showing children how to acquire knowledge about healthy pets.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Parents can encourage their child to research their favorite pets at the library or during a supervised internet search"——父母可鼓励孩子去图书馆或在家长陪同下上网查自己喜爱的宠物，选 A；C 项"让孩子独自上网"与原文"supervised"（有人监督）相反。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`,stem:`How can children still benefit even when their family doesn't have a pet?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By learning that raising geese can do much good.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By watching how healthy pets are brought up.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By hearing about their peers' passion for pets.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By visiting friends who have household pets.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "if a child doesn't have a pet in their own household, they can still benefit from all of the above when visiting a friend who does"——家中无宠物的孩子可以去养宠物的朋友家获得同样益处，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What would the new bill in the Ohio legislature require public colleges and universities to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Send would-be students a packet of information.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Update regularly all information on their websites.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Inform prospective students of their yearly enrollment.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Answer questions raised by applicants and their parents.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "A new bill in the Ohio legislature would require state public colleges and universities to send prospective students a packet of information"——该法案要求公立高校向准新生寄送一份资料包，选 A；B 项"定期更新网站信息"是学校目前的做法，不是法案要求。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What can the state only do, according to Adam Matthews?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Oversee private institutions directly.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Follow other states' example.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Supervise all schools consistently.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Regulate public institutions by law.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "He notes that the state can only regulate public institutions in this way"——州政府只能依法监管公立院校，选 D；A 项"直接监督私立机构"与"只能监管公立"矛盾。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What do we learn about Ohio's schools from the passage?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are resourceful.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are competitive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They provide the best opportunity to realize one's dream.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They appeal most to students from other states in the U.S.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Ohio's schools are already competitive. The state attracts more undergraduate students than it sends to colleges out of state"——俄亥俄的学校已有竞争力，选 B；D 项"最吸引外州学生"原文只说本州学生多于外流学生，并非吸引外州学生。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What is the problem with schools providing information on their websites?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Some students waste much time surfing the net.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Students may need to visit too many websites.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Students have to search for the information.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Some students may lack adequate access.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Many colleges and universities currently provide similar information on their websites… But students need to search for the information"——信息虽在网站上，但学生必须自己查找，选 C；D 项"部分学生无法接入"原文未提。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`accurate`,B:`allow`,C:`artificial`,D:`cheating`,E:`deserted`,F:`establish`,G:`extremely`,H:`immediately`,I:`incorrectly`,J:`normal`,K:`observed`,L:`passed`,M:`reminding`,N:`reparable`,O:`resolve`},passages:[t,n,{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1-1`,text:`Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers’ limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1-2`,text:`One solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don’t read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1-3`,text:`The issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1-4`,text:`“Warning fatigue” and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won’t fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction (发生故障) or an accident occur.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p1-5`,text:`Driver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.`}]},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-1`,text:`Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-2`,text:`Protein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-3`,text:`Many of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing U.S. $18.9 billion in 2020.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-4`,text:`But are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-5`,text:`In fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine’s recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-6`,text:`For example, if you’re a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-7`,text:`While fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-8`,text:`In the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated (饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-9`,text:`Later research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.`},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-p2-10`,text:`Now most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants (抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`In a study by the Universities of York and Kyoto, researchers asked participants to look at pairs of photographs and decide which showed a __26__ face and which showed a person wearing a mask.`,answer:`J`,explanation:`"decide which showed a __26__ face and which showed a person wearing a mask"——该空修饰名词 face，需形容词表示"真人的脸"与"戴着面具的脸"相对，即 normal（正常的），选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-2`,quote:`In a study by the Universities of York and Kyoto, researchers asked participants to look at pairs of photographs and decide which showed a __26__ face and which showed a person wearing a mask.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`The 20% error rate __27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an __28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`"The 20% error rate __27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent…"——过去分词作后置定语修饰 error rate，意为"研究中观察到的 20% 错误率"，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-3`,quote:`The 20% error rate __27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an __28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`The 20% error rate __27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an __28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`"people would struggle to tell an __28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab"——与 real thing（真脸）相对的是"人工/人造的"脸，artificial face，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-4`,quote:`The 20% error rate __27__ in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an __28__ face from the real thing outside of the lab.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`The researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to __29__ any differences according to race.`,answer:`F`,explanation:`"The researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to __29__ any differences according to race"——研究目的是"确定/查明"是否存在种族差异，establish，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-5`,quote:`The researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to __29__ any differences according to race.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`When trial participants were asked to choose between photographs of faces of a different race from theirs, response times were slower and selections were 5% less __30__ .`,answer:`A`,explanation:`"response times were slower and selections were 5% less __30__"——跨种族选择时准确度更低，less 后接形容词 accurate，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-6`,quote:`When trial participants were asked to choose between photographs of faces of a different race from theirs, response times were slower and selections were 5% less __30__ .`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`There are now dozens of criminal cases in which offenders have __31__ themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender, sending police investigations down the wrong path.`,answer:`L`,explanation:`"There are now dozens of criminal cases in which offenders have __31__ themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender"——罪犯"冒充"他人，pass oneself off as 意为"假装成"，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-7`,quote:`There are now dozens of criminal cases in which offenders have __31__ themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender, sending police investigations down the wrong path.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`In one recent case, an international gang used an __32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister, __33__ business executives out of millions of pounds.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`"an international gang used an __32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister"——修饰形容词 realistic 需副词，意为"极其逼真的"面具，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-8`,quote:`In one recent case, an international gang used an __32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister, __33__ business executives out of millions of pounds.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`In one recent case, an international gang used an __32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister, __33__ business executives out of millions of pounds.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`"used an extremely realistic mask to pose as a French minister, __33__ business executives out of millions of pounds"——cheat sb. out of sth. 意为"骗走某人的某物"，此处指假扮法国部长骗走商界高管数百万英镑，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-9`,quote:`In one recent case, an international gang used an __32__ realistic mask to pose as a French minister, __33__ business executives out of millions of pounds.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`Dr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: “Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may __34__ the key characteristics of a person’s appearance to be __35__ identified.`,answer:`B`,explanation:`"super realistic masks may __34__ the key characteristics of a person's appearance to be __35__ identified"——allow sth. to be done 结构，意为"使某人的关键特征得以被识别"，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-10`,quote:`Dr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: “Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may __34__ the key characteristics of a person’s appearance to be __35__ identified.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`Dr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: “Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may __34__ the key characteristics of a person’s appearance to be __35__ identified.`,answer:`I`,explanation:`"may __34__ the key characteristics of a person's appearance to be __35__ identified"——使用逼真面具会让特征被"错误地"识别，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-cp-11`,quote:`Dr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: “Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may __34__ the key characteristics of a person’s appearance to be __35__ identified.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Acquisition of new knowledge may not guarantee the progress that you aim to make.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`C 段 "new knowledge does not necessarily drive new results… learning something new can actually be a waste of time"——学新知识未必带来新成果，只是浪费时间，与"获取新知识未必保证你想要的进步"一致，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-C`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Your current situation in life is built on the beliefs and habits you have been practicing daily.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "The state of your life right now is a result of the habits and beliefs that you have been practicing each day… It is not the things we learn nor the dreams we visualize that determine our results, but rather the habits that we practice each day"——今天的处境由每天实践的信念与习惯塑造，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Knowing the best way to learn a foreign language will not enable you to speak it without actual practice.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "Passive learning creates knowledge. Active practice creates skill." 及 "the only way to build strength is to practice lifting weights"——只知道最好的学外语方法并不能开口说，只有练习才能生成技能，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`People may find it demanding to create a goal which will take a long time to achieve.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`A 段 "trying to set achievable goals that you'll work for over the course of many years can be extremely challenging"——设定需多年努力才能达成的目标非常困难，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-A`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Learning and practicing seem to be very much alike, but the two processes can lead to quite different outcomes.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "Learning something new and practicing something new may seem very similar, but these two methods can have profoundly different results"——学习与练习看似相似，结果却大不相同，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Absorbing new information is helpful in making better decisions prior to action.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`K 段 "soaking up new information can help you make more informed decisions when you do decide to take action"——吸收新信息能帮助你在采取行动前做出更明智的决定，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-K`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Sometimes we may feel like we are making progress by doing some research or making preparations, but actually we are staying at the starting point.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`G 段 "we often claim that we are preparing or researching the best method, but these rationalizations allow us to feel like we are moving forward when we are merely spinning our wheels"——以为在"做研究"其实只是原地打转，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-G`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`We are inclined to think that a lack of knowledge prevents us from advancing to a better future.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`B 段 "It can be easy to assume that the gap between where you are now and where you want to be in the future is caused by a lack of knowledge"——人们常以为差距源于知识不足，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-B`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Learning can benefit you by itself, but only practice can turn what you learn into meaningful contributions.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`I 段 "practice is the only way to make a meaningful contribution with your knowledge… Learning by itself can be valuable for you, but if you want to be valuable to others, then you have to express your knowledge in some way"——学习本身有益，但只有练习才能让知识产生实际贡献，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-I`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Practice involves repeating a process intentionally to attain a particular objective.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "It all comes down to the difference between learning and practicing… 'When we practice something, we are involved in the deliberate repetition of a process with the intention of reaching a specific goal'"——练习是有意图地重复某一过程以达成特定目标，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2024-12-s3-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from research in human-vehicle interaction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Automatic driving systems are by no means immune to errors.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Driverless vehicles are likely to be misused by some people.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Self-driving car manufacturers are not aware of the legal matters involved.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) There is a long way to go before humans can interact with driverless vehicles.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第 1 段 "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof"——人机交互研究显示自动驾驶系统远非万无一失，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What is the problem with requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) End users, young and old alike, find the terms complex to interpret.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Most end users sign them without bothering to read the terms.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Many people are often confused by the wording of the terms.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Most end users do not understand the terms after reading them through.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第 2 段 "The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs"——协议信息不足、且多数用户根本不读，故对买家构成问题，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What would drivers do when they suffer from “warning fatigue”?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Waste no time keeping the car moving.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Rest a while to avoid fatigue driving.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Take no action despite repeated warnings.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Take note of the message though fatigued.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第 4 段 "a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction"——面对"警告疲劳"驾驶员会无视提醒，行车中提示还会造成分心，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author think of continuing to ask buyers to sign end-user license agreements?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will probably not guarantee the safety of drivers in case of accidents.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It likely won’t ensure that the automatic driving system functions properly.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It likely won’t fully protect automakers against accusations of deliberate cheating.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It will probably not provide manufacturers adequate protection from legal responsibilities.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第 5 段末 "All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all"——作者认为在这些问题下自动驾驶前路并不平坦，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What should be done to help drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Training them to be experts in vehicle automation.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Familiarizing them with the systems through training.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Broadening their knowledge of accident-prevention mechanisms.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Facilitating their access to the information provided by the seller.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第 5 段 "Driver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase"——应通过驾驶员培训（而非仅购车时告知）帮助用户充分理解系统能力与局限，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about publicity over protein in America?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It has helped to create the myth of protein deficiency.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It has helped Americans in search of protein-rich diets.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It has raised a lot of health concerns among the public.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It has been funded by food companies and health experts.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第 3 段 "Many of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts"——所谓"蛋白质缺乏"是食品公司与营养专家制造的迷思，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`Why have physicians in the U.S. never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Americans can purchase many kinds of protein-rich foods everywhere.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Americans can absorb the number of calories recommended by food experts.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Americans can get sufficient protein just by eating adequate foods daily.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Americans can rely on food supplements to prevent protein deficiency.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第 4 段 "Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein"——美国医生从未真正诊治过蛋白质缺乏患者，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What should people take into consideration in deciding on the most preferable protein intake?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) How healthy they are and what food they eat.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) How old they are and what activities they do.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) How much protein they consume and what their body weight is.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) How serious their protein deficiency is and what remedies they use.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第 6 段 "Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer"——过量摄入会加重肾脏肝脏负担并增加患病风险，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What foods have been largely out of favor for more than a century?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Sugar-free snacks.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Protein-rich foods.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Both red and white meats.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Sugary and fatty foods.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第 8 段 "protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods"——上世纪以来焦点一直在低卡低脂零食上，蛋白粉退居其次，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author imply people in developed countries should do to keep healthy?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Enjoy natural food.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Eat vegetarian diets.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Consume enough protein daily.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Replace meals with protein bars.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第 10 段 "When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants, vitamins and many other benefits of real food"——以蛋白棒代餐会失去真实食物中的抗氧化剂与维生素，选 A。`,score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`In recent years, China's new energy vehicle industry has developed rapidly. At present, China's annual output of new energy vehicles has reached nearly ten million, accounting for more than 60% of the global market, while exports continue to hit new highs. By increasing investment and providing policy guidance, the Chinese government encourages new energy vehicle manufacturers to pursue technological innovation and continuously improve the competitiveness of their products in the market. The development of China's new energy vehicle industry has not only given a strong boost to the domestic economy, but also made a positive contribution to the use of new energy and environmental protection worldwide.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2024-12-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`近年来，中国新能源汽车产业发展迅速。目前，中国新能源汽车年产量已高达近千万辆，占全球市场份额超过60%，出口量不断创出新高。中国政府通过加大资金投入和政策引导，鼓励新能源汽车企业进行技术创新，不断提高产品在市场上的竞争力。中国新能源汽车产业的发展不仅有力推动了国内经济发展，也为全球新能源利用和环境保护做出了积极贡献。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"近年来，中国新能源汽车产业发展迅速"译作 In recent years, China's new energy vehicle industry has developed rapidly；"目前，中国新能源汽车年产量已高达近千万辆，占全球市场份额超过60%，出口量不断创出新高"用 At present, China's annual output of new energy vehicles has reached nearly ten million, accounting for more than 60% of the global market, while exports continue to hit new highs，注意 account for 与 while 的并列连接。②"中国政府通过加大资金投入和政策引导，鼓励新能源汽车企业进行技术创新，不断提高产品在市场上的竞争力"译作 By increasing investment and providing policy guidance, the Chinese government encourages new energy vehicle manufacturers to pursue technological innovation and continuously improve the competitiveness of their products in the market。③"中国新能源汽车产业的发展不仅有力推动了国内经济发展，也为全球新能源利用和环境保护做出了积极贡献"译作 The development of China's new energy vehicle industry has not only given a strong boost to the domestic economy, but also made a positive contribution to the use of new energy and environmental protection worldwide。全文以一般现在时为主，数据表达（近千万辆、超过60%）需用 nearly ten million、more than 60% 准确呈现，并注意 not only... but also... 的并列结构。`,score:15}]}]}};export{r as default};